  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above normal temperatures and humidity \ncontinues through Tuesday. Relief from the heat & humidity \narrives for the weekend.\n\nAfter a brief respite from the humidity this morning, dewpoints\nwill start climbing back into the upper 60s to low 70s once the\nsun comes up. Temperatures should be a tad cooler today.... \nroughly 85-89 instead of the 90+ we've been seeing lately. \nHumidity will linger through Tuesday, but both temperatures and \ndewpoints will slowly begin to trend lower throughout the week, \nbringing much needed relief to southern New England by Friday. \nHigh temps heading into the weekend likely near 80 with \ndewpoints dropping into the 50s, possibly even the 40s in the \nhigher elevations.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather continues this week with \nshower and thunderstorms chances returning this evening as well \nas Wednesday night/Thursday morning.\n\nA passing cold pool across northern New England should help to \ngenerate CAPE values of 750-1,250 J/kg by late this afternoon. \nCombined with a one-two punch from a passing warm front and \ntrough axis moving through southern New England, some scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms could pop later this evening. CAM \nguidance is once again struggling to resolve these storms, with \nsome models indicating storms over NW MA, others show W CT, and \nothers still keep storms confined to the Albany/NYC metro \nregions. Confidence is on the lower end as far as timing and \nplacement, but currently thinking late afternoon into the sunset\nhours with highest chances for a strong to severe storm forming\nalong the CT River Valley. A few showers could stray as far \neast as the ORH-PVD corridor; however, if these showers move \nthat far east, they should be sub- severe.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday should remain dry and mostly sunny, \npleasant days. But by Wednesday night/Thursday morning, a low \npressure system sitting over the Hudson Bay will be pushing \nsouth/southeast into southern Quebec and New England. A few \nassociated upper-level shortwaves and a cold front will likely \nbe the catalyst for another round of thunderstorm chances; \nhowever, the main limiting factor will be the time of day the \nfront pushes through. If the front hits the eastern MA coast by \nearly Thursday afternoon, we'd expect weaker instability and a \nlower thunderstorm risk. However, if the front stalls or moves \nslower, then the risk for storms would increase, and so would \nthe chance for any one storm to reach severe levels.",
